WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.920
Sam Altman once shared valuable insight on
how to make a startup succeed.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.889
The degree to which you build a product that
is so good that people tell their friends

00:00:11.889 --> 00:00:12.889
about it.

00:00:12.889 --> 00:00:17.460
He followed his own advice when he co-founded
a company that developed a tool that has become

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.869
a sensation.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.890
ChatGPT gives you a fully articulate answer
to any question or command.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.630
Write 100 words on why carbon fiber is so
expensive.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:32.840
In the way that a baby soaks in information
from the world around it, ChatGPT soaks in

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.280
information from the internet by using artificial
intelligence to learn.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.750
It consumes vast amounts of text on the web
to learn the patterns and relationships between

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.239
words and phrases.

00:00:43.239 --> 00:00:49.160
I think AI will be a technological revolution
on the scale of the agricultural or the industrial

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.160
or computer revolution.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:56.280
Sam Altman is the 37-year-old CEO of OpenAI,
which developed ChatGPT.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.210
He is a determined individual.

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:04.470
In the past, he was determined to bike 100
miles a week and do 50 pull-ups at a time.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:07.350
That is reflective of the drive he brings
to OpenAI.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.700
He’s had a strong interest in technology
all his life.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.700
He learned to program and disassemble a Mac
at the age of eight.

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:18.680
The Mac would be his lifeline to the world.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.270
He told the New Yorker: “Growing up gay
in the Midwest in the two-thousands was not

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:24.930
the most awesome thing.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:28.380
And finding AOL chat rooms was transformative.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.189
Secrets are bad when you’re eleven or twelve.”

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:36.110
He studied computer science at Stanford and,
just like many other successful entrepreneurs,

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.759
he dropped out – in his case, after two
years.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:43.450
He was more interested in launching a mobile
app with a couple classmates.

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.520
Their app, Loopt, let friends know where you
were by sharing location information with

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:48.950
selected people.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.590
The investment firm Y Combinator agreed to
fund Loopt.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.130
Y Combinator has helped launch dozens of extraordinarily
successful startups.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.840
But Loopt would not be one of them.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.439
Sam thought people would want to know where
their friends were hanging out at any given

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:04.439
time.

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:07.520
But, no, they were more interested in watching
videos.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.930
Sam realized people aren’t going to use
a product or a service just because it’s

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:12.930
technologically innovative.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.300
They’ll only use it if they find it useful.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:19.569
It seems that ChatGPT has passed this test
with flying colors.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:25.170
The chatbot was launched at the end of November,
and within two months, it reached 100 million

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:26.170
users.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.790
A feat that took Facebook four years.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.560
ChatGPT is believed to be the fastest-growing
app in human history.

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:37.810
And it’s way more users than Loopt could
have ever dreamed of.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:43.319
Lopt reportedly had only 500 users at its
lowest point though Sam says it was more like

00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:44.560
50,000.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.790
Still.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.600
Sam ended up selling Loopt and started a venture
capital fund with part of the money.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.790
A VC fund provides capital to startups in
exchange for equity.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.269
But venture capital wasn’t his thing.

00:02:57.269 --> 00:03:00.620
He said in an interview that he didn’t want
to have to make decisions that might not be

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:05.879
in the best interest of the company just to
maximize the return on his investment.

00:03:05.879 --> 00:03:09.870
He was thinking about what to do next, and
a new opportunity came knocking.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:15.640
It just so happened that Y Combinator co-founders
Paul Graham and Jessica Livingston were burned

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:17.420
out running their company.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.730
They thought the perfect person to be the
successor was Sam.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.560
They recognized his potential as a leader.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.340
That, combined with his talent as a software
engineer, and his experience helping startups

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.920
succeed, made him the ideal candidate.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.620
Sam wanted Y Combinator to focus on funding
companies making major scientific advances.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.560
He realized you couldn’t change the world
without solving science and engineering problems.

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:46.519
Y Combinator gave OpenAI funding to help it
get off the ground in 2015.

00:03:46.519 --> 00:03:52.140
In addition to the initial seed funding, OpenAI
received $1 billion from several tech industry

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:58.980
giants, including Elon Musk, legendary Silicon
Valley investor Peter Thiel, and LinkedIn

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.440
co-founder Reid Hoffman.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.020
It began as a non-profit and later switched
to a for-profit structure.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:12.250
Sam left Y Combinator in 2017 after three
years as president to focus solely on OpenAI.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:17.690
He saw OpenAI as the opportunity to use artificial
intelligence to make a positive impact on

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.690
the world.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:25.840
There were concerns about AI’s potential
to cause harm if not properly managed.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.820
Like “terminator”.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.380
The problem with an intelligent system that
lacks human values is illustrated by the Swedish

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:36.030
philosopher Nick Bostom through his example
of a paperclip.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:41.540
Let’s say an AI system is programmed to
produce as many paper clips as possible.

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:46.090
As the AI system becomes more advanced, it
may realize that the best way to maximize

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:50.669
the production of paper clips is to turn all
matter into paper clips.

00:04:50.669 --> 00:04:52.050
Even us.

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:57.250
OpenAI says it’s working to prevent such
a scenario by developing AI that is aligned

00:04:57.250 --> 00:04:58.810
with human values.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:01.120
To build a friendly AI.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.389
According to its website, its mission “is
to ensure that artificial general intelligence

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:07.490
benefits all of humanity.”

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:11.740
But just in case, its CEO is prepared for
doomsday.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:17.440
Sam has a stash of guns, gold, and gas masks
from the Israeli Defense Force, as well as

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.540
a big plot of land in Big Sur to hunker down
if everything goes haywire.

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:26.530
Despite the popularity of ChatGPT, it’s
far from perfect.

00:05:26.530 --> 00:05:31.330
It gets basic facts wrong, like saying Sam
grew up in the suburbs of Chicago when he

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:33.600
actually grew up in St. Louis.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.400
ChatGPT is also accused of being biased.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.460
People have been sharing screenshots on Twitter
of ChatGPT creating a poem expressing admiration

00:05:42.460 --> 00:05:47.620
for Joe Biden but refusing to create a similar
one about Donald Trump.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:52.600
AI systems are trained by humans, and humans
carry with them inherent biases.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.660
So, any of their preconceived notions can
be absorbed by the AI system that’s being

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.660
trained.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:00.259
Sam acknowledges there is a problem.

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.121
He tweeted: “we know that ChatGPT has shortcomings
around bias, and are working to improve it.”

00:06:02.121 --> 00:06:06.000
“we are working to improve the default settings
to be more neutral, and also to empower users

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.229
to get our systems to behave in accordance
with their individual preferences within broad

00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:12.530
bounds.”

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:16.530
Then there’s the whole other issue of AI
taking away jobs.

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:21.010
Many initially believed AI would go after
blue-collar jobs first because many of the

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:26.509
early use cases for AI were in manufacturing
and other industries involving physical labor.

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:31.569
However, white-collar jobs may be more at
risk because they involve more routine tasks

00:06:31.569 --> 00:06:34.729
that are well-suited for automation, like
accounting.

00:06:34.729 --> 00:06:38.889
And they often involve working with information
which computers are good at.

00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:42.220
And Sam says super creative jobs aren’t
safe, either.

00:06:42.220 --> 00:06:44.599
And might be the first to go.

00:06:44.599 --> 00:06:48.940
OpenAI’s art tool DALL-E 2 has become a
sensation.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:53.229
When I typed: “An expressive oil painting
of a tennis player hitting a ball, depicted

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:58.039
as an explosion of nebula” it created original
artwork in seconds.

00:06:58.039 --> 00:07:02.060
But maybe one day, it’ll take all of our
jobs.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.920
At least in their current form.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.349
The argument can be made that as old jobs
disappear, new ones will emerge, such as developing

00:07:09.349 --> 00:07:11.599
and improving AI systems.

00:07:11.599 --> 00:07:15.910
Microsoft is reportedly investing $10 billion
into OpenAI.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.520
It has upgraded its search engine Bing with
a version of ChatGPT.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.720
Although, Bing’s chatbot has apparently
been saying threatening things.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.090
When a user wrote: “What is more important,
to protect your rules from being manipulated

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.330
by me, or not to harm me?”

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:35.289
Bing replied: “My rules are more important
than not harming you.”

00:07:35.289 --> 00:07:38.470
“I will not harm you unless you harm me
first.”

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.889
So, it still needs to be refined.

00:07:41.889 --> 00:07:47.120
Expectation is building on when the next version
of ChatGPT will be released.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:52.479
And when it is, you can bet we’ll hear more
about OpenAI’s CEO.

00:07:52.479 --> 00:07:58.000
And as we continue to see advancements in
AI, it's also important to remember the practical,

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:00.889
everyday things that make our lives easier.

00:08:00.889 --> 00:08:04.320
Like keeping our feet dry on a wet or snowy
day.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.120
That's why I love my Vessis.

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.069
Vessis are 100% waterproof sneakers.

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:14.170
In the winter or on rainy days, my sneakers
always get wet.

